A||N||3||WhiteFieroBlur||Cooling and Overheating trouble||||9||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||06-11-2000||03:59 PM||||Well I worked on my car yesterday and checked my radiator fluid and guess what? Stupid me forgot to put the cap on. So I drive home for just like 10 min and my car I guess it overheated. The fan stays on when I turn the car on. So I go and check the front. It is completely cold. The temperature gauge shoots past the red to the other side everytime I turn the car on. It goes back to zero when I turn it off. I see some smoke coming from the engine. So I let it sit till the morning.<P> OK I wake up the next moring. Turn on the car so it can warm up. I look at the back and see the same smoke coming from the engine. So I figure I can make it to work so I drive down the street and I hear very loud banging coming from under my driver side, so I pull it over. I look at the back and its steaming like crazy. So I pop the trunk and the back radiator cap has steam shooting out from it. I check the back radiator hose in the back engine and it completely empty, no pressure what so ever. All I hear is air when i sqeeze it.  <P> Could it be because I need to add more radiator fluid to it cause it all spilled out or what? some people say take the thermostat out and maybe its the water pump. But the car starts up fine and everything. it seems all I need to get pressure back into the system cause the back hose it just empty. I do know that I have a leak in my cooling system, its leaking from where that blue dingy thing is onto my passanger floor. ||||reg||9
Z||000001||My7Fieros||06-11-2000||05:51 PM||||Well if you left your cap off, then yes, Im sure its prob. pretty empty. I'd just go on and empty out whats left (in any), then refill and burp the system.<BR>You may want to check your coolant sensor unit too...they can s**t themselves, as well as the stat. None of those parts cost much and the sensor is simple to replace.<BR>As far as the leaking inside the car on the pass side by the "blue thingy", that sounds like the heater core has gone bad. That to is pretty cheap and relativly easy to replace. Odd it would go out at the same time though....||||reg||1
Z||000002||WhiteFieroBlur||06-11-2000||07:07 PM||||Well this was a relative easy fix, I am so glad. I just filled the system with more fluid cause it was indeed empty. :d||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||06-11-2000||07:30 PM||||Sounds like the heater core problem is not sudden but has been leaking for awhile. NEVER remove the thermostat and drive it. it WILL overheat everytime. after it is there for a reason.||||reg||1
